{{4. This is correct you are not maximizing this at the moment for a number of reasons.}}
{{5. The main is overloaded, and it should probably have been set a bit higher based on our recent measurements, logging the Apms the batteries pull at almost fully charged capacity. But note}}
{{that this has not been the issue with previous installations where the company actually gave us charging cycle graphs of battery charging performance. You did not, and therefor as I wrote in}}
{{my email; I could make an educated guess. But the main being to weak, is a fixable problem, but not the worst problem you have.}}
{{6. This is just plain wrong. There is no faulty earth. There are earth fault indicators on all circuit breakers, and they will trip immediately if an earth fault is detected. The problem is a phase}}
{{distortion of the neutral leader as I will go into more detail later in this email.}}
{{7. Main wires to the kWh meter and main are following the guidelines provided by Elvia themselves. (https://www.elvia.no/proff/nettilknytning/maler/krav-til-malermontasje) In the NEK400 /}}
{{IEC standard it says that a 63A breaker needs 16mm2 cables, but this does not however include inside the fusebox itself. They follow the REN standard. 10mm2 cables with reference}}
{{installation method E (NEK400) also have a conductivity of 83A, so this is not a fault.}}
{{8. Cable covers in the main where taken off for the installment of the kWh meter that was suppose to happen the next day. Unfortunately this has been rescheduled many times, not to our fault.}}
{{9. This is correct, we have yet to mark the main fuse, and we have yet to install an overview of the circuit breakers. Awaiting the final documentation.}}
{{10. Some of the lines installed have not been fixated to the ground, this is because we are waiting on the shelves that you where supposed to set up. We have talked about this and agreed that}}
{{the current placement is not optimal, and therefor also not properly secured yet. You know this. Also the cables used can be classified as flexible cables, and does not need fastening all the}}
{{way if we decide to conform to that solution.}}
{{11. We don't deliver these documents until we have received payment. It's our "security deposit".}}
{{12. This also have to do with the charge cycle of the batteries. We did not expect them to charge at "full speed" all the way to 100%. But again, this is the problem that is the easiest to fix.}}
{{Lets talk about your issue. I don't know what other companies you got to have a look at the installation, but their conclusions are unfortunately way off.}}
{{The problem is that you can only use 50-60% of the rated capacity because the chargers you have plugged in, are basically like a PC charger and converts the the 230V AC to 36V/48V DC. When}}
{{they do this, they distort the phase compensation of the neutral leader. (N-leader) due to a lot of induced noise in the electrical system.}}
{{Ex. If the other phases are balanced perfectly the current flowing in the N-leader should be ∑0. Usually they are not perfectly balanced, and to calculate the current flowing in the N-leader we use}}
{{the following formula:}}
{{∑Amps(N)=√(A^2+B^2+C^2-AB-AC-BC)}}
{{The test we conducted on a few of the lines connected in the first sub distribution box where as following: (A)L1: 10A (B)L2: 11A (C)L3: 12A. We can see that they are pretty balanced, but not}}
{{entirely, so the ∑Amps(N) is going to be >0. So if we plug in the numbers into the formula we get that ∑Amps(N)=1,73A this is what current should be flowing in the N-leader, and this would be}}
{{perfectly fine. The current we measured was 18,6A!! this is enough to make the 16A breaker trip, when only loading it with 75%.}}
{{The noise produced by the chargers have a measured level THD (Harmonic distortion) of 129%. The limit for Norwegian electronic products are 4%... This is the main issue with the installation, and}}

